Lab 20: Deploying Scalable Applications with OpenStack
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Deploy scalable applications using OpenStack Heat (Orchestration Service)
Configure autoscaling policies for dynamic workload management
Deploy a multi-tier application with scaling and monitoring capabilities
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, ensure you have:

Access to an OpenStack cloud environment with admin privileges
OpenStack CLI tools installed (openstack, heat)
Basic understanding of YAML syntax
Familiarity with OpenStack core services (Nova, Neutron, Heat, Ceilometer)
Lab Setup
Install OpenStack CLI tools if not already present:
sudo apt-get install python3-openstackclient python3-heatclient
Source your OpenStack RC file to authenticate:
source ~/openrc.sh
Task 1: Using Heat for Scalable Deployment
Subtask 1.1: Create a Basic Heat Template
Create a new file named basic_stack.yaml with the following content:
heat_template_version: 2018-08-31

description: Basic scalable web server deployment

resources:
  web_server:
    type: OS::Nova::Server
    properties:
      name: web_server
      image: cirros
      flavor: m1.small
      networks:
        - network: private
Deploy the stack:
openstack stack create -t basic_stack.yaml basic_stack
Expected Outcome: A new stack named basic_stack will be created with a single web server instance.

Subtask 1.2: Verify Stack Deployment
Check stack status:
openstack stack list
View stack resources:
openstack stack resource list basic_stack
Troubleshooting Tip: If the stack fails, check logs with:

openstack stack event list basic_stack
Task 2: Configure Autoscaling Policies
Subtask 2.1: Create Autoscaling Template
Create a new file autoscale_stack.yaml with scaling configuration:
heat_template_version: 2018-08-31

description: Autoscaling web application

resources:
  server_group:
    type: OS::Nova::ServerGroup
    properties:
      name: web_servers
      policies: [anti-affinity]

  scaling_group:
    type: OS::Heat::AutoScalingGroup
    properties:
      min_size: 1
      max_size: 3
      resource:
        type: OS::Nova::Server
        properties:
          name: web_server_%index%
          image: cirros
          flavor: m1.small
          networks:
            - network: private
          user_data: |
            #!/bin/sh
            echo "Web server ready" > /tmp/ready

  scale_up_policy:
    type: OS::Heat::ScalingPolicy
    properties:
      adjustment_type: change_in_capacity
      auto_scaling_group_id: { get_resource: scaling_group }
      cooldown: 60
      scaling_adjustment: 1

  scale_down_policy:
    type: OS::Heat::ScalingPolicy
    properties:
      adjustment_type: change_in_capacity
      auto_scaling_group_id: { get_resource: scaling_group }
      cooldown: 60
      scaling_adjustment: -1
Subtask 2.2: Deploy Autoscaling Stack
Launch the autoscaling stack:
openstack stack create -t autoscale_stack.yaml autoscale_stack
Monitor the group scaling:
watch -n 5 openstack stack resource list autoscale_stack
Key Concept: The cooldown period prevents rapid scaling fluctuations by enforcing a wait time between scaling actions.

Task 3: Deploy Multi-Tier Application with Scaling
Subtask 3.1: Create Multi-Tier Template
Create multi_tier_stack.yaml with web and database tiers:
heat_template_version: 2018-08-31

description: Multi-tier application with scaling

resources:
  database:
    type: OS::Trove::Instance
    properties:
      name: app_db
      datastore_type: mysql
      flavor: m1.small
      volume: { size: 10 }

  web_servers:
    type: OS::Heat::AutoScalingGroup
    properties:
      min_size: 2
      max_size: 5
      resource:
        type: OS::Nova::Server
        properties:
          name: web_node_%index%
          image: ubuntu-latest
          flavor: m1.small
          networks:
            - network: private
          user_data:
            str_replace:
              template: |
                #!/bin/bash
                apt-get update
                apt-get install -y apache2
                echo "Connected to DB: $DB_ENDPOINT" > /var/www/html/index.html
              params:
                $DB_ENDPOINT: { get_attr: [database, host] }

  web_alarm_high:
    type: OS::Ceilometer::Alarm
    properties:
      meter_name: cpu_util
      statistic: avg
      period: 60
      evaluation_periods: 2
      threshold: 70
      comparison_operator: gt
      alarm_actions:
        - { get_attr: [scale_up_policy, alarm_url] }

  web_alarm_low:
    type: OS::Ceilometer::Alarm
    properties:
      meter_name: cpu_util
      statistic: avg
      period: 300
      evaluation_periods: 2
      threshold: 30
      comparison_operator: lt
      alarm_actions:
        - { get_attr: [scale_down_policy, alarm_url] }
Subtask 3.2: Deploy and Monitor
Launch the multi-tier stack:
openstack stack create -t multi_tier_stack.yaml app_stack
Check resource creation:
openstack server list
openstack database instance list
Monitor scaling alarms:
openstack alarm list
Troubleshooting Tip: If database creation fails, ensure the Trove service is enabled in your OpenStack cloud.

